description:
Review finding from operating-level ladder audit. Severity: CRITICAL.

README contract at HEAD says custom tool SQL is loaded only when the classifier proves READ_ONLY, even if a profile permits a higher ceiling; write, DDL, PL/SQL block, and unproven package-call definitions are rejected at load time.

Empirical failing case added only in clean scratch worktree /tmp/oraclemcp-cod6-review-ladder-0a51dcb9, not committed:

```rust
#[test]
fn readme_contract_rejects_write_custom_tools_even_on_writable_profiles() {
    let c = Classifier::new(oraclemcp_guard::ClassifierConfig::new());
    let d = def_sql("bump", "UPDATE t SET x = 1 WHERE id = 7", None);

    let err = classify_at_load(&d, &c, OperatingLevel::ReadWrite)
        .expect_err("custom tools must load only classifier-proven READ_ONLY definitions");

    assert!(matches!(
        err,
        LoadError::OverCeiling {
            required: OperatingLevel::ReadWrite,
            max: OperatingLevel::ReadOnly,
            ..
        }
    ));
}
```

Command run from clean HEAD worktree:
`CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2 CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/home/durakovic/projects/oraclemcp/target/cod6-review-ladder cargo test -p oraclemcp-core readme_contract_rejects_write_custom_tools_even_on_writable_profiles -- --nocapture`

Actual result: FAIL. `classify_at_load(&d, &c, OperatingLevel::ReadWrite)` returned `LoadedTool { ... required_level: ReadWrite }` for `UPDATE t SET x = 1 WHERE id = 7`.

Do not weaken the loader or soften the README without operator direction. The fix needs to decide the intended security contract and preserve fail-closed behavior.

notes:
RECORD CORRECTION (second agent on this bead). The 14:23 close was premature: the bead was marked closed while its tests were still UNCOMMITTED in the shared working tree, so nothing had actually landed. The tests are now landed in commit 1661dee, and strengthened well past the original version (which asserted only 'final level >= base level' over 5 hardcoded statements).

LANDED (1661dee) — six laws over generated (statement, policy) pairs in crates/oraclemcp-guard/tests/proptest_invariants.rs:
 1. composition never loosens the base verdict — base level carried through unchanged (==, not >=), floor only rises, every predicate attributable to a matched rule;
 2. composition is MONOTONE — splicing extra rules in at any generated position never loosens: a deny can never be reopened, the floor only rises, predicates only accumulate (this is the bead's headline law; the original test did not cover it at all);
 3. rule order carries no authority — rotation-invariant outcome/floor/predicate-set, so there is no first-match/else precedence that could shadow a rule;
 4. an empty policy is the identity, never a grant;
 5. no policy can unlock a Forbidden base — refusal stays attributed to the classifier, not to a rule;
 6. the N3 predicate rewrite cannot launder a statement into a weaker verdict — danger/level never drop, a read-only base stays read-only, and the policy filter must survive into the rendered SQL (SEC-1).

TEETH VERIFIED, NOT ASSUMED: mutating the composer max->min is caught by laws 1+2 with a genuine counterexample (a catch-all read_only floor pulling a DDL drop statement down from DDL clearance to READ_ONLY); reachability probes confirmed the Narrow/Narrow and Reclassified arms are actually exercised, so no law passes vacuously. That counterexample is frozen as the representative adversarial-corpus case policy_composition_corpus_read_only_floor_never_demotes_a_ddl_statement.

GATES: scoped fmt + clippy -D warnings + all 178 oraclemcp-guard tests green.

OPEN AC ITEM (not silently dropped): 'mutation gate >= 90 for the composition crate'. scripts/mutation_safety_gate.sh is the nightly/pre-tag pass — 998 mutants on policy.rs alone, and the script documents a prior 40GB-RSS OOM that killed unrelated processes on this host, so it is not safe to fire unilaterally in a 6-agent shared tree. The graph already routes that re-run to .16 GATE-SEAL, which this bead blocks. Operator's call whether to run it sooner.

